> > On Thursday, April 4, 2024 at 9:06:27 AM UTC-4 Ken Kellner wrote:
> >
> >> The output from this line
> >>
> >> BAEA<-predict(fit_M1,'state', species=”BAEA”)[1,]
> >>
> >> Is the predicted marginal occupancy of BAEA at site 1, which is
> >> calculated based on the specific covariate values for site 1. You don't
> >> show these specific covariate values but they are likely the reason for
> >> what you are seeing.
> >>
> >> When you calculate the marginal effects of covariates like this, you are
> >> holding all covariates except one at their mean values. So all predictions
> >> for all marginal plots are derived from sets of covariate values that are
> >> uniformly "average" except for one covariate which ranges from very low to
> >> very high. Now, suppose that site 1 represents an extreme case and has
> >> "very high" values of all or most covariates, relative to the averages
> >> across sites. In your case perhaps it's in the upper corner of the map and
> >> so has both high latitude and high longitude. That would result in a
> >> prediction which could (depending on the exact model) fall well outside any
> >> of the predictions generated when you were holding all non-focal covariates
> >> at their means.
> >>
> >> If you are not sure about this, plug the actual covariate values for site
> >> 1 into the newdata. You should get the same results (the CIs might not be
> >> exactly identical because they are bootstrapped).
> >>
> >> Ken
> >>
