> >>> model (fit_M1), I use the ‘predict’ function to look at the species
> >>> specific mean marginal occupancy:
> >>>
> >>>  BAEA<-predict(fit_M1,'state', species=”BAEA”)[1,]
> >>>
> >>> #Predicted         SE     lower     upper
> >>>
> >>> #1 0.9546969 0.01118197 0.9292771 0.9698831
> >>>
> >>> My understanding of this output is that these are the predictions across
> >>> all possible occupancy states (0000, 1000, 1100, etc.) at each possible
> >>> site (taking into account each site's covariate values) along with standard
> >>> errors. This is marginalized across all species in the model. Note that the
> >>> marginal mean occupancy for Bald Eagle is high (0.95, range 0.92-0.97).
> >>>
> >>>  I also look at the marginal effect of each covariate (e.g. below for
> >>> latitude)
> >>>
> >>> nd_marg <- data.frame(
> >>>
> >>>   year_st = rep(mean(occ_covs$year_st), 100),
> >>>
> >>>   longitude = rep(mean(occ_covs$longitude), 100),
> >>>
> >>>   latitude = seq(min(occ_covs$latitude),
> >>> max(occ_covs$latitude),length.out = 100))
> >>>
> >>> marg <- predict(fit_M1, type = 'state', species = “BAEA”, newdata =
> >>> nd_marg)
> >>>
> >>> The predicted marginal occupancy for BAEA based on the covariate
> >>> latitude range from 0.58-0.89, marginalized across all other species. I
> >>> notice that in all cases the predicted marginal occupancy values for the
> >>> covariate effects (using a new dataframe for each) are below the predicted
